concepts and features for a programming language tentatively called Hey

three key innovations, of usefulnesses TBD: operator-oriented syntax, scoped implicit variables, bounded for-while loops

tilde ~ for I/O stream transfer looked like a good move, should copy that here

a loop takes its bounds from the left of an at-sign @ and its content from the right
the "while" aspect comes from the conditional break, with the question-mark ? operator: left side condition, right side break-value
with no bounds written, the loop follows (Python) range(0, 1, 1), which yields a "sugar" for if-else expressions:
@(cond?true_val
false_val)
longer if-else chains drop out naturally from this notation:
@(cond1?val1
cond2?val2
cond3?val3
else_val)

a loop's "bounds" may include a function amidst the numbers, which would map each index to a value for the implicit !
a function that returns a (length, indexer) pair would then be an iterable, guaranteed bounded by this indexing property
e.g. each$arr@(!)~stdout would write each element of arr to stdout
complication: what if we want to access both index and value-at-index in the loop?
do we need multiple implicits?

conventional language expresses "loop over all input" with a while-loop, which thus can't be bound in advance, but we're usually fine with that
perhaps a loop bound could also be a sufficiently input-like condition function, to make such a while-loop
what's "sufficiently input-like"? maybe "at most as true as the raw output of a syscall generally accepted to eventually return false", maybe only a small list of functions indicated in the library
aliter, we can keep each code block bounded, but the whole program usefully unbounded, if after the explicit main part of main() we enter a language-level event-handling loop
so a cat program would in either case be readline@(!)~stdout but in the first view that means "while-loop by readline(), for it's limited by input" and in the second view that means "once in the event loop, check regularly for readline() and execute that 'loop' body when it becomes true"
does the event-handling version trivially allow arbitrary while-loops and all their harms? maybe

exclamation-mark ! assigns its RHS to its LHS name as a const, but if there's no LHS, there's no assignment, and if there's no RHS, it fetches an implied variable
it's right-associative

several operators each introduce an implicit variable available to their subexpressions via exclamation-mark ! which variables usually depend on the left and are accessed on the right
implicits from further scopes come from repeated !s
colon : makes the whole LHS implicit, to obviate augmented-assignment operators
at-sign @ makes the loop counter implicit
question-mark ? makes the conditioned value implicit

comparison operator a<b returns (Python) 0 if a >= b else b - a, which makes the implicit variable in a break more useful in some cases

function calls should probably use dollar-sign $ per precedent of Haskell, maybe likewise for array indexing, but indexing should make an implicit variable and calls probably shouldn't

some operators such as slash / return multiple values, of which only the first is used by default, but the rest can be captured with a comma-separated list in a ! assignment

some operators such as colon : don't literally evaluate their LHS
e.g. cond?!:0 evaluates almost like (Python) (cond := False) if cond else None, instead of an error from "0 is not an lvalue"
cond|n<10?!:0 might error from "|-operation is not an lvalue", but fancifully we could take cond|n<10:0 to mean (Python) (cond := 0) if cond else (n := 10), for 10 is the least value making n<10 false

